{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793853327,"nonce":"6MUXlDBdJSYHie1xr6Oi7SwLtv0=","title":"Found while verifying real application code with `warrant verify --csc-args` (after 72d9738, which runs source generators). Milestone: M6 (`9002a2c`), alongside types defined outside the compilation through contract classes. Parent epic: `54c6e06f383039f9758f1571999c683db1c244561561998cf9a52c41b84fa52f`. Related: `a1ee3a7` (closed-class equality versus the CLR's `Equals`).","message":"A `[Verified]` method whose parameter is a closed-set class from a **referenced assembly** is rejected with WRT0002: `M4 does not support type defined outside the compilation: \u003ctype\u003e`. This is common in real code: the closed sets live in a shared library project, and the logic that uses them lives in another project.\n\nThe example that hit it is a pure scoring function, five `bool`s plus a closed set compared once with `==`, which is otherwise squarely in the M5a subset. The set's base class (a `StringClosedSetBase\u003cT\u003e`-style hierarchy) overloads `operator ==` to compare by a `Value` property, so `==` is a user-defined operator, not reference identity.\n\nTwo things are missing.\n\n1. **A closed type from metadata.** The closed shape check (WRT0009) needs source: it checks that every static instance is initialised with its own `new`, and that the constructors are called nowhere else. Metadata shows the private constructors and the static properties of the class's own type, but not the initialisers or the other calls. Options:\n   - Require the referenced assembly to be built with Warrant too, so its own build checks the shape and records the result, for example as an assembly-level attribute naming each closed type and its instances, which the consuming build trusts as a recorded fact.\n   - Accept an external `[Closed]` type as an unchecked assumption, listed in the assumption report (\"`T` has exactly the instances `A`, `B`\").\n2. **A user-defined `==` on a closed class.** The model treats `==` on a closed class as instance identity (KTD11). A value-comparing `operator ==` agrees with identity when every instance has a distinct `Value` and no other instances exist. That needs either a check (distinct values, from the recorded instances) or an assumption, as in `a1ee3a7`.\n\nAcceptance: a `[Verified]` method in project B taking a `[Closed]` set from project A, with a value-comparing `==` inherited from a base class, translates; its theorems prove; and the assumption report says what was checked and what was assumed.","files":null}]}